        "text": "The researcher claimed that students who were exposed to argumentation in the classroom through the dialogical argumentation-based instructional model intervention showed greater improvement in their understanding of the Nature of Science and the Nature of IKS and skills use of meteorological concepts using appropriate resources compared with students who did not experience this particular learning strategy. Zidny and Eilks (2020) also described a study that aimed to combine Indigenous knowledge and Western science in chemistry education through class discussion in Indonesia. The teacher engaged the students on sustainability issues related to pesticide use, comparing conventional pesticides with a green pesticide from Indigenous science. The lesson involved various activities, including exploring students' prior knowledge of pesticides, watching a TEDx video on pesticide risks, analysing an article on Indigenous science perspectives on pesticides, applying chemistry concepts to answer questions, reflecting on personal perceptions and alternative views from Indigenous African Journal of Research in Mathematics, Science and Technology Education science, and engaging in group classroom discussion to express their arguments based on evidence and reasoning. The findings show that argumentation allowed for the exploration and discussion of students' different perspectives, including those related to Indigenous knowledge. These findings are further corroborated by a study conducted at three South African township secondary schools, where teachers were found integrating students' socio-cultural practices, experiences and beliefs through strategies such as group argumentation, probing questions and open-ended inquiries rooted in real-life contexts, students' engagement in authentic problem-based activities and codeswitching between English and students' Indigenous languages to invoke meaningful classroom interactions that allowed the exploration of scientific concepts (Mavuru & Ramnarain, 2020). The findings show that these strategies enhanced the significance and applicability of science to students, supported students' engagement and comprehension of science topics, promoted students' sense of inclusion and identification with knowledge, which enhanced their motivation to learn science, and supported students in reconciling their existing belief systems with newly acquired scientific concepts, which led to an improvement in their understanding of science (Mavuru & Ramnarain, 2020).",
