      "snippet": {
        "text": "It could be worthwhile for educators and researchers to focus on the strategic use of questions as key pedagogical drivers as students' science investigations can be hindered by their teachers' difficulty in helping them to pose researchable questions (Morrison, 2008). So, while teachers may be utilising emergent open-ended questions in their science teaching, they may not yet be fully realising the educational potential of researchable questions for framing science lessons and extending students' science language skills. \n\nMany of the more minor themes, including Classroom Setup (e.g. Hooper & Harmon, 2015), Group Work-Class Discussion (e.g. Ocak, 2010), Reflection-Meta Cognition (e.g. Karaçalli & Korur, 2014), and Scaffolds (e.g. Bulu & Pedersen, 2010), have strong scholarly evidence-bases supporting their positive impact on science learning outcomes. In fact, Literacy Scaffolds (n = 18) and Group Work (n = 25) featured prominently in a recent scoping review of 142 research outputs focusing on primary science programmes enhancing students' science achievement, skills, and attitudes (Deehan et al., 2022). In one instance, linear regression indicated that the utilisation of interactive, conceptual word walls as part of a physical classroom environment in a disadvantaged setting positively influenced the science passing rates and overall science achievement levels of a group of 115 sixth grade learners (J. K. Jackson, 2014). However, the form and function of environmental prompts can vary considerably in practice (Hooper & Harmon, 2015). The absence of argumentation in the data was also noteworthy due to its clear alignment with the aspirations for scientific literacy (Akerson & Bartels, 2023;Roberts & Bybee, 2014) and its increasing prominence in science syllabi (e.g. NESA, 2023). This aligns with findings from a survey 297 science educators' perceptions of relative importance of different aspects of science professional development, wherein argumentation was viewed as one of the least important areas (Burke et al., 2022). Furthermore, teachers have struggled to incorporate argumentation into their science teaching practices (Simon et al., 2006).",
