          "status": "GOLD",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.5539/jel.v14n5p26?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.5539/jel.v14n5p26, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The findings of this study highlight the need for sustained and consistent support to help teachers implement inquiry-based teaching effectively. While PLCs offer a collaborative space for discussing pedagogical strategies, more intentional structures may be required to bridge the gap between theory and practice. Instructional coaching, as a complementary strategy, should be flexible enough to accommodate teachers' diverse schedules and teaching contexts. Offering differentiated coaching tailored to individual classroom realities can foster more consistent engagement with science instruction and better support teachers' professional growth. \n\nSchool leaders play a pivotal role in enabling effective science instruction. The barriers identified in this study suggest a need to rethink scheduling and staffing models to prioritize science teaching across all grade levels. Administrators could benefit from policies that promote interdisciplinary learning, protect instructional time for science, and reduce scheduling conflicts with other subjects. Addressing these systemic challenges may lead to more equitable and sustained access to high-quality science education. \n\nThis study provides a foundation for further exploration into the external factors influencing inquiry-based teaching. Future research could investigate how variations in teacher schedules, access to resources, and student demographics shape the adoption and effectiveness of inquiry-based science instruction. Additionally, employing more robust observational methods and larger sample sizes could capture a more comprehensive picture of classroom dynamics, offering deeper insights into how inquiry-based practices evolve over time and in different educational contexts. \n\nThe study's findings offer valuable insights into how teachers interpret and implement inquiry-based teaching in real-world classrooms. Refining the understanding of what constitutes effective inquiry-based instruction can help develop clearer frameworks for practice. Furthermore, the study underscores the need for improved tools and rubrics that more accurately measure the quality and depth of inquiry-based science teaching. Enhancing these measurement approaches can support teachers in aligning their practices with inquiry-based pedagogy. \n\nIn conclusion, the study underscores the value of PLCs and instructional coaching in supporting the implementation of inquiry-based practices. However, the effectiveness of these strategies is contingent on ensuring that discussions and plans within PLCs are effectively translated into classroom practices. By providing sustained coaching support, fostering collaboration, and ensuring that teachers are equipped with the skills to bridge the gap between theory and practice, schools can create environments where inquiry-based science instruction thrives.",
