        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The diverse applications and implementations of automated feedback systems underscore the need for a systematic and nuanced examination of their practices, methodologies, and impact within programming education. Several prior literature reviews have provided valuable foundations. For example, Keuning, Jeuring, and Heeren (2018) conducted a systematic review of automated feedback tools for programming exercises, proposing a taxonomy of feedback types (such as error feedback vs. style feedback) and emphasizing the importance of aligning feedback with pedagogy. Deeva et al. (2021) reviewed learning analytics-driven feedback systems, shedding light on how data from student interactions can inform personalized feedback strategies. Cavalcanti et al. (2021) focused on intelligent tutoring systems for programming, highlighting advances in AI and natural language processing that enable more interactive feedback dialogues. Most recently, Messer et al. (2024) expanded the scope to include AI-driven and LLM-based feedback, discussing the transformative potential of these technologies as well as challenges such as scalability and fairness. While these reviews each address specific facets (e.g., types of feedback, or particular technologies), the research remains somewhat siloed. The field lacks an integrative overview that brings these strands together to offer a holistic understanding of how automated feedback is being designed, implemented, and evaluated in programming education. In particular, with the recent advent of LLMs in this space, an updated synthesis is required to capture cutting-edge developments and persistent gaps.",
