      "snippet": {
        "text": "The decision to focus on secondary school students is significant because this period represents a crucial transition in science learning. Students at this stage are developing higher-order thinking skills and preparing for advanced education (Suweken, 2024). Therefore, enhancing SCT skills during this time is essential for their cognitive development and academic success (Chernikova et al., 2020). \n\nThe Next Generation Science Standards signify a substantial shift in K-12 science education, emphasizing the importance of STEM fields to prepare students for future challenges and opportunities (Murphy et al., 2017). The Scientific Critical Thinking (SCT) model is an educational framework designed to cultivate critical thinking skills and enhance student self-efficacy (Rusmansyah, 2020). Rusmansyah et al. (2018) demonstrated the efficacy of the SCT model in improving critical thinking and self-efficacy among PGSD students focusing on colloidal material. The SCT model comprises five components: student orientation, scientific activity, presentation of scientific results, completion of critical thinking tasks, and evaluation. This study employs a developmental research approach to evaluate the SCT model's practicality and effectiveness in teaching colloidal material. Additionally, Hasbie et al. (2023) developed science learning modules that significantly enhance critical thinking and self-efficacy among seventh-grade students in junior high school. \n\nThe Program for International Student Assessment (PISA) results from 2000 to 2022 indicate a consistent trend in Thailand's science scores, with averages of 425 (2009), 444 (2012), 421 (2015), 426 (2018), and 409 (2022) (PISA Thailand Promotion Institute for Science and Technology, 2023). Similarly, the 2022 Ordinary National Educational Test (O-NET) results for Grade 9 revealed low science scores: Science (32.50%), Biology (22.84%), Physics (21.06%), Chemistry (26.79%), and Earth Science (33.32%) (National Institute of Educational Testing Service, 2023). These results highlight the urgent need to enhance critical and scientific thinking skills, promoting the use of technology for better information analysis. Conventional teaching methods such as rote learning have led to students' passive learning and inadequate higher-order thinking skills.",
