      "snippet": {
        "text": "In many countries, general schooling ends at year nine or ten, before students enter the senior secondary phase where they specialise in a limited number of subjects. The present study emanated from a project commissioned by the South African quality assurance body, Umalusi, which aimed to benchmark the South African curriculum against the equivalent curriculum in Singapore, British Columbia (Canada) and Kenya. The study was originally conducted in 2013 using the curriculum documents in use at that time. \n\nCurriculum in this study refers to the programme of study for the whole period of schooling, while syllabus refers to the programme of work specifically for biology in a single grade. \n\nIn South Africa and British Columbia, science comprises biology, chemistry, physics and earth and space science from the seventh to ninth years of schooling. British Columbia includes a significant section devoted to processes of science. In South Africa, biology is contained in a strand called Life and Living (Department of Basic Education, 2011), while British Columbia names biology 'Life Science' and selects content according to an organising principle in each year of study. The organising principle for Year 7 is ecosystems, in Year 8 it is cells and systems, and in Year 9 it is reproduction (British Columbia Ministry of Education, 2010b). \n\nKenya and Singapore teach biology within an integrated subject called science in the seventh and eighth years of schooling. In Kenya, science consists of 10 units which incorporate knowledge from biology, physics, chemistry, agricultural science, health education and earth science (Kenya Institute of Education, 2002a). In the ninth year (Form 1), students select two science subjects from Biology, Physics and Chemistry (Kenya Institute of Education, 2002b). Since the ninth year was not equivalent to integrated science offered in South Africa and British Columbia, it was excluded from this study. \n\nIn Singapore, science in the seventh and eighth years (Lower Secondary) is organised according to four multidisciplinary themes, incorporating topics from biology, chemistry and physics. The themes are Diversity, Models, Systems and Interactions. The ninth and tenth years of secondary education in Singapore comprise Upper Secondary, in which Biology is treated as a stand-alone subject (Ministry of Education & University of Cambridge Local Examinations Syndicate, 2013).",
