      "snippet": {
        "text": "The primary purpose of the current study was to use visual analytics to understand better how science literacy learning outcomes are achieved in U.S. schools. This was done by examining 49 school-level variables derived from the student, teacher, and principal questionnaires using the 2015 Programme for International Student Assessment (PISA) 1 Here the term 'perference' is used as a generic term for mapping schools onto variable relationship plot. \n\nC. Ding data. These 49 variables were chosen to represent characteristics of the school learning context, including teaching force and science-specific resources that principals reported, teacher characteristics and perceptions of school teaching practices that teachers reported (e.g., selfefficacy related to teaching science content), and student dispositions towards science learning and teaching practices that students reported (e.g., enjoyment of science learning). Teacher and student variables were aggregated to the school level because both teacher and student data were nested within the school. We attempted to use visual analytics to examine if these variables form a holistic view of how each component works together to support or hinder science literacy outcomes. The information may help school leaders set priorities to enhance schoolwide science literacy achievement. The research question for this study is: \n\nUnder what conditions do schools achieve better learning outcomes concerning science literacy? \n\nThe current work is distinctive for two reasons: First, most schoollevel studies focus on a few variables at a time rather than a large number of variables. Although it is important to examine certain specific variables about science learning (Soltani, 2020), the effects of a few specific variables (e.g., motivating science classes) may not provide the whole picture of the variables' role in school science literacy outcomes. Thus, it is necessary to include as many variables that are part of the learning environment as possible, particularly if school administrators need to consider many factors when making decisions about the school environment to achieve the best science learning outcomes. In doing so, we hoped to determine the most salient components congruent with science literacy outcomes. We recognized that science literacy achievement is an individual student characteristic, and most variation is between students in the same school. However, there is also substantial, reliable variation between schools (Ma et al., 2018). From a policy perspective, actions can be taken to improve science literacy outcomes in a school.",
