      "snippet": {
        "text": "The student experience in high school career academies includes integrating college preparatory curricula with career and technical education courses, aligned with students' career interests. The career academy model is strengthened by input from business and industry leaders connected with the career theme through an advisory board as well as faculty from postsecondary education representing associated college majors (Castellano et al. 2017;Kemple and Snipes 2000). \n\nWhile schools which closely adhere to the components of the career academy model are likely to have better learning experiences and student outcomes, the adherence to the career academy model and quality of implementation of its practices have widely varied across schools and districts (Kemple and Snipes 2000). However, NAF is a non-profit organisation that helps to improve school stakeholders' implementation of the academy model and adhere to their standards of practice (Stern, Dayton, and Raby 2010). NAF assists schools in the implementation of industry validated curricula, professional development of teachers, and examination of performance data to inform evidence based practices for a national network of high school career academies within five career themes: engineering, finance, health sciences, hospitality and tourism, and information technology (National Academy Foundation 2014). NAF also conducts quality review visits on an ongoing basis to assess and evaluate the level of implementation for their academies. Their reviews are based on academies' fidelity to NAF standards of practice. The academies are classified as: certified (meeting core components), model (showing strong evidence of model fidelity), and distinguished (evidence of fidelity above the threshold of a model academy) (National Academy Foundation 2014). These data are important to consider given prior research demonstrating wide variations in programme implementation across career academies (Kemple and Snipes 2000). \n\nTo better understand the student experience based on varying levels of adherence to the NAF model, the purpose of this research project was to examine the impact of school implementation levels based on three stages of fidelity (certified, model, distinguished). More specifically, we compared academy students' engagement in college and career readiness activities to non-academy comprehensive high school students. The following research questions guided our study. . \n\n(1) How do career academy students from schools with different levels of model fidelity compare to their traditional, comprehensive high school counterparts with regard to engagement in college readiness activities;",
