        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Adaptive feedback and adaptive learning broadly refer to computer-based learning in which student data is used to inform the instruction offered, leading to improved learning outcomes; these may also be referred to as intelligent tutoring systems (Mavroudi et al., 2018;Shute & Towle, 2003;VanLehn, 2011). Adaptive learning has been shown to be more effective than comparable nonadaptive designs (Kulik & Fletcher, 2016;Ma et al., 2014;VanLehn, 2011). The reason for this effectiveness is the way that adaptive learning helps students to recognize and correct their own misunderstandings-in much the same way that a human tutor promotes learning. In fact, meta-analyses have found some computer tutoring to be as effective as human tutoring (Ma et al., 2014;VanLehn, 2011). \n\nThe iVFT described here uses relatively simple adaptive designs that provide feedback specific to the student's responses and decisions. Simple though it is, this adaptive feedback allows students to experience the complexity of a field site while also being able to rely on immediate and appropriate feedback if and when they get stuck. This safety net has allowed us to design a learning experience that places students in an active role in an inquiry-driven exploration of a field site. \n\nTo demonstrate the effectiveness of our model of iVFTs as powerful learning experiences, we will present evidence of student attainment of learning outcomes in two settings that have used one of our iVFTs: in-person, Advanced Placement high school classes and fully online general education undergraduate classes. The study will also describe the approach to learning design used in creating this and other iVFTs. Finally, in the main text and the supplemental materials, we provide detailed information to support educators in using this iVFT in their own classrooms.",
