        "text": "The close links among these three areas have provided efficient contexts for gathering data about students' learning activities. The role of computer sciences has also been over emphasised in the research (Lee et al., 2020); significant data has been gathered on developing algorithms and finding new ways to analyse data from different online platforms. However, the use of learning analytics within elementary and secondary level schools is a relatively underresearched area (Valtonen et al., 2024). Altogether, learning analytics for supporting teaching and learning has shown potential in elementary and secondary level school classrooms. However, these potentials and expectations have not yet been met (Kovanovic et al., 2021), even with the use of different blended learning approaches using different technologies. This research focuses on this gap by studying elementary and secondary level teachers' perceptions and uses of learning analytics. The aim is to show teachers' feelings about present and future applications of learning analytics applications in their classrooms. \n\n2 Theoretical Background Ye (2022) indicates that the starting point of learning analytics can be seen in the early versions of Computer Assisted Instruction (CAI), which included simple drill and practice rehearsal software designed to reinforce learning through repetitive exercises focusing on specific content areas, such as mathematics (Koschmann, 1996). These systems later evolved towards more adaptive and personalised learning solutions. These tools, which can collect user data and provide simple visualisations of students' progress via dashboards, have become highly popular in everyday school activities (Kovanovic et al., 2021). Typically, these applications provide students with pre-defined sets of tasks or learning paths and gather information about the progress of studies. While some tools offer general learning paths that are similar for all students, others can be seen as more smarter systems offering intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) and adaptive environments and providing different, more personal paths with different materials and tasks based on the data collected from each student (Koschmann, 2012;Ye, 2022). These systems are grounded in principles of behaviourism and information processing theory (Koschmann, 2012), with the progression and activities of learning determined by the software (Ye, 2022).",
