Title: Intelligent science exhibits: Transforming hands-on exhibits into mixed-reality learning experiences
Authors: Nesra Yannier, Kevin Crowley, Youngwook Do, Scott E. Hudson et al.
Year: 2022
Venue: The Journal of the Learning Sciences
Citations: 17
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6089a6e410b99d08a1334b59f99c4387ee0cfd8d
TL;DR: Evidence is found that the Intelligent Science Exhibit produces substantially better learning for both scientific and engineering outcomes, equivalent levels of self-reported enjoyment, and higher levels of engagement as measured by the length of time voluntarily spent at the exhibit.

[Paper 2]
Title: AI Adaptivity in a Mixed-Reality System Improves Learning
Authors: Nesra Yannier, Scott E. Hudson, Henry Chang, K. Koedinger
Year: 2024
Venue: International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education
Citations: 16
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0e84d19e17ad7261606e88eb7d894bdc91bb29bc
TL;DR: This paper introduces AI adaptivity in the context of a new genre of Intelligent Science Stations that bring intelligent tutoring into the physical world and shows that adaptivity using Bayesian Knowledge Tracing in the context of a mixed-reality system leads to better learning of scientific principles, without sacrificing enjoyment.

[Paper 3]
Title: Automated Feedback Can Improve Hypothesis Quality
Authors: K. Kroeze, S. M. van den Berg, A. Lazonder, B. Veldkamp et al.
Year: 2019
Venue: Frontiers in Education
Citations: 9
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/698a0f7219546df5dccdc09a3ed53478ee29cf55
TL;DR: An automated parsing and feedback system that informed students about the quality of hypotheses they had created in an online tool, the hypothesis scratchpad, and found that the subset of students in the experimental condition who asked for automated feedback on their hypotheses were much more likely to create a syntactically correct hypothesis.

[Paper 4]
Title: The use of scaffolding to promote preschool children’s competencies of evidence-based reasoning
Authors: Ilonca Hardy, Simone Stephan-Gramberg, Astrid Jurecka
Year: 2021
Venue: Unterrichtswissenschaft
Citations: 6
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8f7d010e36d7779d192d8ddfa0ef16b5e5bc8fa8
TL;DR: It is found that adaptive prompts with modeling significantly improved children’s evaluation of irrelevant events in the posttest and suggest that preschool children are able to reason with evidence if they are given adequate support.

[Paper 5]
Title: Power to the Teachers: An Exploratory Review on Artificial Intelligence in Education
Authors: Petros Lameras, S. Arnab
Year: 2021
Venue: Inf.
Citations: 217
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e3b6d42ab070a148086478bb980a6a30c4d16176
TL;DR: This exploratory review attempted to gather evidence from the literature by shedding light on the emerging phenomenon of conceptualising the impact of artificial intelligence in education by utilising the PRISMA framework to review the analysis and synthesis process encompassing the search, screening, coding, and data analysis strategy of 141 items included in the corpus.

[Paper 6]
Title: A Web-based System to Support Inquiry Learning - Towards Determining How Much Assistance Students Need
Authors: B. McLaren, Michael J. Timms, D. Weihnacht, Daniel Brenner et al.
Year: 2014
Venue: International Conference on Computer Supported Education
Citations: 1
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/38c0d1894e11a451bd8e2677dd02592e0e916982
TL;DR: Higher performing students gainfully use the Voyage to Galapagos program’s support more frequently than lower performing students, those who arguably have a greater need for it, and also that higher performers generally are better at asking for help.

[Paper 7]
Title: Personalized and Adaptive Learning
Authors: Deborah L. Taylor, M. Yeung, A. Bashet
Year: 2021
Venue: Innovative Learning Environments in STEM Higher Education
Citations: 81
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b561bc1b901e0510824314ab0247c3c766af2551
TL;DR: One goal of this chapter is to define adaptive and personalized learning as it is used at this time in the hopes that as the technology evolves the promise of increased student learning can come to fruition.

[Paper 8]
Title: ScratchThAI: A conversation-based learning support framework for computational thinking development
Authors: Kantinee Katchapakirin, Chutiporn Anutariya, T. Supnithi
Year: 2022
Venue: Education and Information Technologies : Official Journal of the IFIP technical committee on Education
Citations: 29
URL: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b42d7ac7de95f76c073eb4b8b75b69d49556e9b
TL;DR: This research proposes and develops ScratchThAI as a conversation-based learning support framework for computational thinking development to support both students and teachers and indicates positive teacher satisfaction, better learning performance and higher student engagement.

