        "text": "During inquiry learning, experiments can be implemented differently, for example, as hands-on or virtual experiments (cf. de Jong et al., 2013). Hands-on experiments are performed with concrete, physical materials and (measuring) devices. Virtual experiments, on the other hand, are interactive computer simulations that allow students to actively manipulate specific variables and observe the consequences in the simulation (de Jong, 2006;de Jong & van Joolingen, 1998). In the simulation, dynamic data visualization, multiple representations of the data and guidance or feedback can be included. Virtual experiments can be used to appropriately implement inquiry learning and have been shown to foster understanding of science concepts (de Jong, 2006;de Jong & van Joolingen, 1998;Geelan & Fan, 2014). Brinson (2015) conducted a literature review on non-traditional (virtual and remote) versus traditional (hands-on) experiments. He found that in most studies reviewed (89%) non-traditional experiments led to higher or equal learning outcome achievement compared with traditional experiments. \n\nWhereas virtual experiments can be easily implemented also in a remote schooling scenario, hands-on experiments are usually not accessible at home due to lack of material and apparatus. An alternative to hands-on experiments for remote learning are video experiments (Sever et al., 2013). Video experiments are recordings of demonstration experiments, which are an established part of traditional science education (Sever et al., 2010). In demonstration experiments, teachers perform the experiment in class, with students observing the experiment (Trna & Trnova, 2015). Video experiments can be easily recorded by any teacher with access to the experiment's materials and measuring devices and then made available to students to prepare for class, as a homework assignment, or as instruction during remote schooling.",
