      "snippet": {
        "text": "Automated feedback systems are predominantly implemented in higher education (81.97%), particularly within online learning formats (68.85%). This reflects historical research focus in postsecondary computing education and the practical advantages of these tools for scaling instructional support in large or asynchronous courses (Marin et al., 2017). The typical integration within courses indicates their growing role as core instructional components beyond mere supplement tools, facilitating continuous assessment and personalized guidance at scale (Reigeluth et al., 2015) While this integration into higher education marks an important step toward mainstream adoption, the limited use in K-12 and blended classroom settings reveals significant gaps in both research and practice. The underrepresentation of primary and secondary education suggests that existing systems may not yet align with the developmental, curricular, or logistical needs of younger learners and school-based instruction. Furthermore, the dominance of fully online delivery leaves open questions about how automated feedback can be adapted for use in face-to-face or hybrid contexts, where in-person teaching and classroom interaction may call for more flexible or complementary feedback roles. \n\nEffective implementation into diverse educational contexts requires intentional design and implementation strategies. This includes not only technical integration but also pedagogical alignment with instructor practices, curricular goals, and student readiness (Keuning et al., 2018;Kazemitabaar et al., 2024). Teacher implementation support is particularly critical; while many systems are integrated into course structures, fewer explicitly describe how instructors are trained, supported, or involved in the feedback loop. Future work could focus on robust professional development, customizable feedback settings, and clear instructional guidance to promote meaningful uptake and sustained use (Keuning et al., 2018;Kazemitabaar et al., 2024).",
