      "snippet": {
        "text": "Prior to the widespread adoption of LLMs, researchers explored methods to design interactive computer agents for tutoring students with crafted templates [33] or integrating latent semantic analysis methods [31]. Advances in LLMs have driven significant shifts in mathematics education across both K-12 and higher education settings [21]. LLMs assist students by providing detailed explanations [15,41,46,108] or asking Socratic questions [2,3,25] to facilitate their problem-solving, offering personalized guidance to their learning plan [83,114], and identifying and correcting their mistakes [41,105,109]. Systems such as ChatTutor [17] integrate these efforts and aim to offer a comprehensive tutoring process, covering informative instruction, course plan adjustment, quiz offering and evaluation, and more. On the other hand, LLMs also support teachers by designing teaching materials [37,45], generating exam questions [50,70], and grading assignments [10,65]. Finally, platforms such as Khanmigo [1] provide tools and services facilitating both student learners and teachers. Educators and researchers have noted that LLM-based applications can help overcome common challenges in mathematics education, such as limited teacher resources [24], by offering readily available feedback and scaffolding. Besides, recent studies show that LLM-based agents can simulate student personas and offer interactive, personalized support in education, thereby enabling an even more adaptive learning environment. For example, Markel et al. [63] simulated students in office hours and applied the system for real-life teaching assistants to receive valuable teaching practices. Xu et al. [104] collected a dataset of real students' learning behaviors and proposed Eduagent to simulate these behaviors, such that educators can have a more precise modeling and prediction of the students' learning dynamics. To effectively assess the quality of designed questions, Lu and Wang [59] constructed LLM-simulated students to answer these questions. Finally, in the domain of mathematics education, Lyu et al. [61] examined the usage of teachable AI agents to facilitate \"learning by teaching\", where students learn by teaching the AI agents. Their work found that the personalities of AI agents have an impact on the students' learning outcomes.",
